---
import Legend from '../components/Legend.astro';
import { editions, formatEditionDate, quadrants, rings } from '../data/radar';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { evidenceMeta } from '../lib/evidence';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="What the ITK Dev Tech Radar is, how to read its rings and quadrants, and how to propose a change."
  section="about"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="page-head__title">How to read the radar</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede">
        The radar is ITK Dev's shared opinion of the technologies we build with. It helps us choose consistently across
        projects, and shows newcomers and partners what to expect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-page">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What it is, and what it is not</h2>
      <p>
        Each technology, tool or technique on the radar is a <em>blip</em>. Its position says how strongly we recommend
        it for new work, and every blip has a short write-up explaining why. The radar is based on our own experience
        and on the code in our public GitHub organisation. It is not a list of everything we use: settled,
        uncontroversial technology is left off unless it helps to say it out loud.
      </p>
      <p>
        The radar is advice for choosing technology, not a rule that overrides a project's context. If a project has a
        good reason to go another way, write that reason down, ideally as an architecture decision record in the
        project.
      </p>

      <h2>Rings</h2>
      <p>The closer a blip is to the centre, the more confident we are in it.</p>
      <dl class="definition-list">
        {rings.map((ring) => (
          <div>
            <dt>{ring.name}</dt>
            <dd>{ring.description}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <h2>Quadrants</h2>
      <p>Quadrants only group blips by kind. Which quadrant a blip lands in matters much less than its ring.</p>
      <dl class="definition-list">
        {quadrants.map((q) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              <a href={`${base}${q.id}/`}>{q.name}</a>
            </dt>
            <dd>{q.description}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <h2>Markers</h2>
      <Legend />
      {editions.length === 1 && (
        <p>
          This is the first edition, so there is no movement to show yet. From the next edition, new blips and blips
          that moved between rings get their own marker.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>Editions</h2>
      <p>
        We publish a new edition each time the team has reviewed the radar together. Each blip keeps the history of its
        placements, with a note for every edition, so you can see how our view has changed. Blips that are no longer
        relevant are taken off the radar.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {editions.map((edition) => (
          <li>
            Edition {edition.id}, {formatEditionDate(edition.published)}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Usage figures</h2>
      <p>
        Each blip shows how many repositories in the{' '}
        <a href={`https://github.com/${evidenceMeta.org}`}>{evidenceMeta.org}</a> organisation use it. A script checks
        every public, non-archived, non-fork repository with recent activity for a signal, such as a Composer package, a
        configuration file or a Docker image, and stores the result with the radar. Private repositories are not
        included, so the figures show how widespread something is, not exact totals. The figures inform the placement
        but do not decide it: something can be widely used and still on hold.
      </p>

      <h2>Propose a change</h2>
      <p>
        Anyone at ITK can propose a new blip or argue for moving one. <a href={site.proposeUrl}>Open a proposal</a> on
        GitHub with the technology, the ring you suggest and your reasoning. Proposals are discussed at the next radar
        review. Changes to the write-ups themselves are made as pull requests to{' '}
        <a href={site.repoUrl}>the radar repository</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
